<item>
  <title>Does the NBA Actually Have a Tanking Problem?</title>
  <dc:creator>Tyler Ahlberg</dc:creator>
  <link>https://your-website-url.example.com/posts/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>In May, the National Basketball Association took an unprecedented step in how it handles its amateur draft. The NBA reformed its draft lottery system to directly punish the three worst performing teams with only a 5.4% chance at the first overall pick, while teams that finish four through ten will have a 8.1% chance. Followed by positions 11-14 with a 5.4% chance and 15-16 with a 2.7% chance. This marks the first time one of the “big four” North American sports leagues hasn’t outright rewarded the worst performing teams with the top pick or give them the most favorable odds in a lottery system for the amateur draft.</p>
<p>NBA commissioner Adam Silver would tell you the reason for these drastic changes are because of the practice of “tanking.” Tanking involves a team intentionally losing games with the goal of securing a top pick in the amateur draft, a process that was modernized in the mid 2010s by former Philadelphia 76ers executive, Sam Hinkie. The man behind the infamous “Trust the Process” mantra, though tanking has been present in the NBA for decades with disgraced former Los Angeles Clippers owner Donald Sterling being recorded on tape advocating to lose games for Virginia star Ralph Sampson. Now between the extended history of tanking and the recent lottery reforms the question becomes, does the NBA actually have a tanking problem?</p>
<p>For this alleged technique of intentionally dropping games to truly be fruitful for those engaged, teams would have to be pretty confident in the level of difference maker a top pick represents.</p>
<p>Looking from the 2004 NBA Draft to the 2018 NBA Draft, or 15 total years of drafting, one can see how slanted the level of talent is, relative to the draft position by win shares. Win shares is a metric that combines stats that measure offensive contribution such as points and assists with stats that measure defensive contribution such as steals and opponents’ field goal percentage. While not a perfect stat if you’re trying to discern the better of two players, it does a good job showing which players have been a part of winning teams and have had meaningful contributions on those teams.</p>
