<p>The NBA initially instituted the NBA draft lottery system in 1985, when famed Georgetown center Patrick Ewing was selected first overall by the New York Knicks via the drawing of an (allegedly frozen) envelope. From 1985 to 2018, the lottery saw a pretty standard system of teams receiving better odds the worst their record, aside from adjustments in the early 1990s. Specifically, the worst team in the NBA had a 25% chance at the top pick and the remaining 75% distributed among the other 13 teams, with higher odds going to teams with worse records</p>
<p>This changed in 2019 after the aforementioned “Trust the Process” era which former 76ers general manager Sam Hinkie was very transparent about his “process” with something of an Al Capone-esque flaunting of his strategy to the media. Under the new system, the three worst teams all had an equal 14% chance at the first overall pick, a substantial decrease from the 25% for the team with the worst record as it was in the previous system. The reform also flattened the odds for teams positioned 4-14, with the fourth worst team having a 12.5% chance, the fifth-worst with 10.5% and slowly decreasing for teams with better records. This change was meant to deter teams from tanking via a lottery system that didn’t allow the bottom teams to consistently secure premiere picks, but did this work?</p>
