<p>In the eight years before the 2019 lottery reform, the worst team in the league didn’t receive less than a top two pick a single time. However, in the eight seasons post-reform, the worst performing team only got a top two pick three times.</p>
<p>Mechanistically, the 2019 reform did its job: it hurt the value of tanking because teams were no longer a shoo-in for a top two pick even with the league’s worst record. Yet it asks the question, in spite of a rather hitting of the target by the NBA with the 2019 lottery reform, did the reform actually deter teams from tanking?</p>
<p>The answer? Sort of.</p>
