<p>Looking at five seasons before the reform and five seasons after the reform, after their initial bottom-five finish, both groups had the same amount of teams still remaining in the bottom five after two seasons with six each. By the third and final year tracked in the bottom five, the post-reform group actually have less teams hanging around at the bottom than the pre-reform group.</p>
<p>So it seems that the 2019 lottery overhaul to deter tanking was effective in its ability to steer bad teams away from getting what had previously been almost a guaranteed top 2 pick while simultaneously keeping teams from sticking around the bottom of the pile at a better rate than the years before the reform. Yet we sit here months after the most drastic anti-tanking measure in the history of North American sports. Why? Does the NBA have a tanking problem? It doesn’t seem like it. The measures taken to deter tanking seem to have worked at comparable if not better rather than before the 2019 lottery reform.</p>
<p>Now we sit here in November 2026, the Sacramento Kings sit at a measly 1-10 record and are set to be punished for it with worse odds at acquiring top talent. Why introduce another reform when the previous one seemingly worked? In the modern NBA bad teams are set to be punished for the first time in the history of the league, discontinuing a staple of not only the NBA, but North American sports at large. The attempted establishment of parity via an even distribution of the top amateur talent every season. The NBA has created a purgatory for bad teams.</p>
<p><img src="https://your-website-url.example.com/posts/Purgatory.png" class="img-fluid" style="width:70.0%"></p>



